#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Settings: one window for the settings of the FlickOS desktop.

Each FlickOS setting has one owner, the command that stores and applies it:
flickos-layout (layout, style, desktop clicks), flick-tiler (tiling),
flickos-shortcuts (the shortcut sheet), flickos-screenshot (screenshots and
recordings) and flickos-countme (the weekly count).
The pages here are a view over those
commands: they read with `state`, `list` or `status` and change things by
running the same commands a user could type. This program never writes their
files. A page reads when it is shown, in the background (Ui.background), so
the window opens at once and a slow command never freezes it.

flickos-control owns the mouse, touchpad and keyboard settings itself (the
SETTINGS table): `set` saves one in ~/.config/flickos/input or
~/.config/flickos/keyboard (system defaults: /etc/xdg/flickos/input.conf and
keyboard.conf) and turns them into two files in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/flickos/
control/: labwc.xml (<libinput> device categories and <keyboard> options) and
environment (the keyboard layout as XKB_DEFAULT_* variables). flickos-layout
merges them into its overlay (rc.xml, labwc/environment) and reloads labwc.
`prepare` writes them at login, before flickos-layout prepare (flickos-session).
labwc never resets an option that disappears from its config, so every option
that was set stays written with its value, and "default" stores the default.

Screen zoom (Accessibility; labwc's magnifier, whose keys are in flickos-
settings' rc.xml) is a setting of the same kind: ~/.config/flickos/zoom,
turned into a <magnifier> section of labwc.xml.

The language and the formats (dates, numbers, money) are settings of the same
kind: ~/.config/flickos/locale, turned into LANG and LC_* lines of the same
environment file, so apps started from then on use them.

The layout of the login screen and the console is /etc/default/keyboard; the
Keyboard page changes it through pkexec and /usr/libexec/flickos/control-helper.
The Login, Users and Firewall pages (autologin, user accounts, ufw), the power
button and the lid (Power & Idle; a logind drop-in) and adding a language
(locale-gen) use the same helper. The system language itself goes through
`localectl set-locale`, which asks for the password (polkit).

Night light (Displays; on by default, and how warm) and Do Not Disturb
(Notifications) are settings of the same kind. `night-light` (autostart)
runs flickos-settings' night-light script, which starts wlsunset for the time
zone, with the chosen warmth, or nothing while it is off. Do Not Disturb is
mako's do-not-disturb mode (flickos-settings' mako config hides all but
critical notifications in it); `notifications` (autostart) turns it on again
once mako runs, since a new mako starts without it.

Startup applications are XDG autostart entries (~/.config/autostart, then
autostart in XDG_CONFIG_DIRS): labwc doesn't run them, so `startup run`
(autostart) does, and the Startup Applications page and `startup
enable|disable|add|remove` change the user's entries. flickos-settings hides
the entries of the programs its autostart starts itself
(/etc/xdg/flickos/autostart).

Animations (Accessibility) are GSettings' enable-animations, which GTK and
FlickOS's pop-ups read: `animations set` changes it with gsettings and
records the change; GSettings stays where it is stored.

Automatic updates are unattended-upgrades' apt settings, read with
`apt-config dump`; the Updates page changes them through the helper
(`updates`, a drop-in in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d).

History: every change of a setting is recorded by the program that owns it
(this one, flickos-layout, flick-tiler, flickos-screenshot) in $XDG_STATE_HOME/flickos/
history.jsonl. The History page and `history` show them and undo one by
running its owner's command with the old value, only while it is the
setting's newest change and the setting still holds its new value. This
program records system settings (`system`, which the pages run), default and
startup applications and user accounts the same way. Software changes come
from apt's own log, through the helper (packages-list), which also checks and
does their undo. history.enabled (on by default) stops recording and clears it.

Screen arrangements (Displays) are kanshi profiles in ~/.config/flickos/
displays: `displays save` keeps the arrangement of the screens connected now
(made in wdisplays), and kanshi, started by autostart through `displays
start` only while a profile is kept, applies it whenever the same screens are
connected.

Settings that have their own app (network, sound, displays, ...) are tiles on
the More Settings page that start the app. Wayland has no way to show another
app's window inside this one.

The search box above the pages finds a page, a setting (the rows and headings
the pages build, collected while the window opens) or one of the other apps
(the tiles), by their words and SEARCH_KEYWORDS; a result opens the page at
that row, or starts the app.

The window is a GTK 3 window (python3-gi), so it follows the Arc-Dark or Arc
theme of the chosen style; it has no CSS of its own. GTK is imported only when
the window opens, so `list` works without a display.

Commands:
  (none)             open the window; --page ID opens it on that page
  list               print the pages: id, name
  get [KEY]          print settings: key, value, where it comes from
  set KEY VALUE      change a setting and apply it ("default" for the default)
  prepare            write the labwc fragments for this login (flickos-session)
  idle               run swayidle with the chosen lock and suspend times (autostart)
  lock               lock the screen with the chosen background (keys, menus, swayidle)
  night-light        run night light as chosen, or nothing while it is off (autostart)
  notifications      turn Do Not Disturb on again once notifications run (autostart)
  startup list       print the startup applications: id, on/off, user/system, name
  startup enable|disable|remove ID
                     turn one on or off from the next login, or remove the user's own
  startup add APP    start an installed app (its desktop file id) at every login
  startup run        start the startup applications that are on (autostart)
  updates            print the automatic update settings
  displays list      print the kept screen arrangements: name, screens
  displays save      keep the arrangement of the screens connected now
  displays forget NAME|all
                     forget a kept arrangement
  displays start     run kanshi for the kept arrangements, if any (autostart)
  displays arrange   open wdisplays; a screen it turns on over another moves beside them
  about              print the system information (the About page, for bug reports)
  history [--days N] [--json]
                     print the changes to the desktop's settings, newest first:
                     id, time, undo (or - when it can't be undone), what changed
  history undo ID    put a setting back the way it was before that change; a
                     software change (its id is a date and time) is undone
                     through the helper, which checks it first
  system KEY [VALUE] print or change a system setting through the helper or
                     timedatectl (asks for the password), recorded for History:
                     power.power-key|lid|lid-external-power, firewall,
                     autologin, updates.auto|scope|reboot, timezone, ntp,
                     keyboard.system (layout or layout(variant))
  default-app list   print the default applications: category, app id, name
  default-app set CATEGORY APP
                     open CATEGORY (browser, files, editor, images, documents,
                     media, terminal) with an installed app
  history restore WHEN [--dry-run]
                     put every setting changed since WHEN back as it was then
                     (not system settings or software): WHEN is a change's id
                     (just before it), a date (the end of that day) or a date
                     and time; one entry of its own, whose undo puts them back
  history clear      forget the changes kept so far

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 error, 2 usage error.
"""

